  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\n**Description**\n\nThere appears to be a discrepancy between the WAI-ARIA 1.2 specification and the HTML Living Standard regarding the naming requirements for the `<table>` element.\n\nIn ARIA 1.2, the role definition for table explicitly indicates \"Name Required: true\", meaning that an element with role=\"table\" (or an element with an implicit table role) must be given an accessible name. This can be provided via a `<caption>`, aria-label, or aria-labelledby.\n\nHowever, in the HTML Living Standard, the `<caption>` element is optional, even though `<table>` has an implicit role=\"table\". This results in a situation where an HTML `<table>` without a caption, aria-label, or aria-labelledby has no accessible name, thereby conflicting with ARIA 1.2’s requirement that all tables have one.\n\n**Expected Alignment**\n\nGiven that `<table>` has an implicit ARIA role of table, both specifications should ideally align on naming requirements. This could be achieved by either:\n\nUpdating the HTML specification to note that `<table>` should have an accessible name via` <caption>`, aria-label, or aria-labelledby, or\nClarifying in the ARIA specification that the \"name required\" rule applies only to explicitly assigned role=\"table\" elements, not those with an implicit role.\n\n**Rationale**\n\n- Ensuring alignment between the HTML and ARIA specifications would:\n- Improve clarity for authors on when naming is required.\n- Prevent potential false positives or confusion in accessibility testing tools.\n- Reinforce consistent accessibility guidance across W3C and WHATWG standards.",
